{"surah": 104, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "وَيْلٌ لِّكُلِّ هُمَزَةٍ لُّمَزَةٍ ", "translation": "Woe to every scorner and mocker", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَيْلٌ", "translit": "ٌlْyَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِّكُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُkِّl", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "هُمَزَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَzَmُh", "root": "همز"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لُّمَزَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَzَmُّl", "root": "لمز"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "ٱلَّذِى جَمَعَ مَالًا وَعَدَّدَهُۥ ", "translation": "Who collects wealth and [continuously] counts it.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "جَمَعَ", "translit": "َʿعَmَjg", "root": "جمع"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَالًا", "translit": "āًlāَm", "root": "مول"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَعَدَّدَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَdَّdَʿعَw", "root": "عدد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "يَحْسَبُ أَنَّ مَالَهُۥٓ أَخْلَدَهُۥ ", "translation": "He thinks that his wealth will make him immortal.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَحْسَبُ", "translit": "ُbَsْḥَy", "root": "حسب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَالَهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَlāَm", "root": "مول"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَخْلَدَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَdَlْhkَaʾ", "root": "خلد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "كَلَّاۖ لَيُنۢبَذَنَّ فِى ٱلْحُطَمَةِ ", "translation": "No! He will surely be thrown into the Crusher.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "كَلَّا", "translit": "āَّlَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَيُنۢبَذَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَhdَbۢnُyَl", "root": "نبذ"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْحُطَمَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَmَṭُḥْlٱ", "root": "حطم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "وَمَآ أَدْرَىٰكَ مَا ٱلْحُطَمَةُ ", "translation": "And what can make you know what is the Crusher?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَدْرَىٰكَ", "translit": "َkٰàَrْdَaʾ", "root": "دري"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْحُطَمَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَmَṭُḥْlٱ", "root": "حطم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "نَارُ ٱللَّهِ ٱلْمُوقَدَةُ ", "translation": "It is the fire of Allāh, [eternally] fueled,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "نَارُ", "translit": "ُrāَn", "root": "نور"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْمُوقَدَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَdَqwُmْlٱ", "root": "وقد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 7, "text": "ٱلَّتِى تَطَّلِعُ عَلَى ٱلْأَفْـِٔدَةِ ", "translation": "Which mounts directed at the hearts.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلَّتِى", "translit": "àِtَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَطَّلِعُ", "translit": "ُʿعِlَّṭَt", "root": "طلع"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْأَفْـِٔدَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَdِٔـْfَaʾْlٱ", "root": "فاد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 8, "text": "إِنَّهَا عَلَيْهِم مُّؤْصَدَةٌ ", "translation": "Indeed, it [i.e., Hellfire] will be closed down upon them", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّهَا", "translit": "āَhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِم", "translit": "mِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مُّؤْصَدَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَdَṣْwʾُّm", "root": "وصد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 9, "text": "فِى عَمَدٍ مُّمَدَّدَةٍۭ ", "translation": "In extended columns.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَمَدٍ", "translit": "ٍdَmَʿع", "root": "عمد"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مُّمَدَّدَةٍۭ", "translit": "ٍۭẗَdَّdَmُّm", "root": "مدد"}], "hasIhya": false}], "tafsir": {"1": [{"arabic": "الصيام إن كانا صائمين وعن مجاهد أنه قال في ويل لكل همزة لمزة الهمزة الطعان في الناس واللمزة الذي يأكل لحوم الناس وقال قيادة ذكر لنا أن عذاب القبر ثلاثة أثلاث ثلث من الغيبة وثلث من النميمة وثلث من البول وقال الحسن والله للغيبة أسرع في دين الرجل المؤمن من الأكله في الجسد وقال بعضهم أدركنا السلف وهم لا يرون العبادة في الصوم ولا في الصلاة ولكن في الكف عن أعراض الناس وقال ابن عباس إذا أردت أن تذكر عيوب صاحبك فاذكر عيوبك وقال أبو هريرة يبصر أحدكم القذى في عين أخيه ولا يبصر الجذع في عين نفسه وكان الحسن يقول", "english": "Salim ibn Jabir said: \"I came to the Prophet, peace be upon him, and said: 'Teach me something beneficial by which I may profit.' He said: 'Do not belittle anything of goodness, even if it be that you pour from your bucket into the vessel of one who seeks water, and that you meet your brother with a cheerful face. And if he turns away, do not backbite him.'\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 681", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 681: On Backbiting (Ghibah) — Its Severe Prohibition and the Narrations Concerning It", "section_index": 681, "anchors": "- Root: غيب (ghayb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of being hidden, concealed, absent from sight.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الغيب: مصدر غابت الشمس وغيرها: إذا استترت عن العين\" — used for what is absent from the senses; the backbiter (mughtab) speaks of the absent person in a way that defames him.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"تكلم بذلك عن ظهر الغيب\" — speaking about one who is absent. Metaphorical (Majaz): The term \"ghibah\" is transferred to the act of mentioning a person's fault in his absence, as established by the Hadith.\n- Root: لحم (lahm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Flesh, meat; \"لحمت العظم: أخذت ما عليه من اللحم\" — to strip flesh from bone.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اللحم جمعه: لحام، ولحوم\" — and the Quranic prohibition {أيحب أحدكم أن يأكل لحم أخيه ميتا} [Al-Hujurat: 12] employs the metaphor of eating a dead brother's flesh for backbiting.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"معه لحمان كثير ولحام\" — having much meat. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"رجل لحيم: قتيل، وقد لحم ومعناه قطع لحمه\" — and \"أكل لحوم الناس\" is the classical rhetorical expression for backbiting, as in the Hadith of the two women who \"ate the flesh of people.\"\n- Root: عور (awr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The private parts, a blemish, a weak point; \"عورة\" — that which one conceals.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Related to \"عور\" — exposing what is hidden and shameful.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"عورة\" — the exposed, vulnerable part. Metaphorical: \"تتبع عورات المسلمين\" — seeking out their hidden faults and sins, as in the Hadith: \"من تتبع عورة أخيه تتبع الله عورته.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The Hadith \"حتى أسمع العوانق في بيوتهن\" demonstrates the periodic structure where the prepositional phrase \"في بيوتهن\" is interposed after the verb \"أسمع\" to emphasize the far-reaching effect of the Prophet's voice, showing the permissibility of such interposition when the meaning remains clear and the sentence is well-formed.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Insight"}], "6": [{"arabic": "أما بعد فإن الغضب شعلة نار اقتبست من نار الله الموقدة التي تطلع على الأفئدة وإنها لمستكنة في طي الفؤاد استكنان الجمر تحت الرماد ويستخرجه الكبر الدفين في قلب كل جبار عنيد كاستخراج الحجر النار من الحديد وقد انكشف للناظرين بنور اليقين أن الإنسان ينزع منه عرق إلى الشيطان اللعين فمن استفزته نار الغضب فقد قويت فيه قرابة الشيطان حيث قال {خلقتني من نار وخلقته من طين} فإن شأن الطين السكون والوقار وشأن النار التلظي والاستعار والحركة والاضطراب ومن نتائج الغضب الحقد والحسد وبهما هلك من هلك وفسد من فسد ومفيضهما مضغة إذا صلحت صلح معها س", "english": "In the name of God, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. Praise be to God, upon whose pardon and mercy none rely except the hopeful, and from whose evil wrath and assault none fear except the fearful — He who gradually seizes His servants from whence they know not, and who has given dominion over them to their lusts, commanding them to abandon what they desire, and who has tried them with anger while charging them to swallow their rage in what provokes them. Then He surrounded them with hardships and pleasures, and granted them respite to see how they would act. He tested their love through them, to make known the truth of what they claim. He made known to them that nothing is hidden from Him of what they conceal or proclaim, and warned them lest He seize them suddenly while they are unaware. For He said: {«They await but a single blast which will seize them while they are disputing; so they will not be able to make a bequest, nor to their families will they return»} (Q. 36:49-50). And prayers and peace be upon Muhammad, His Messenger, beneath whose banner the prophets walk, and upon his family and companions — the rightly-guided imams and the pleasing masters — prayers whose number equals the number of what has been of God's creation and what will be, and by whose blessing the first and the last are favored — and abundant peace.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Envy", "section_title_ar": "Section 700", "section_title_en": "The Book of Condemnation of Anger, Spite, and Envy — Book Five of the Quarter of Destroyers from The Revival of the Religious Sciences", "section_index": 700, "anchors": "- Root: غضب (ghadab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of anger, rage, fury; the boiling and agitation of the blood\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance — when attributed to God, it means retribution (intiqām) alone, not emotional agitation; the Prophet described it as \"a live coal kindled in the heart of the son of Adam\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical — literal anger is the boiling of the blood in living beings; metaphorical usage extends to inanimate things like fire and the reins of a mount\n- Root: قلب (qalb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The heart; also the turning, overturning, and transformation of a thing from one state to another\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance — the heart is so named for its constant turning (taqallub) and fluctuation between states; it is the seat of understanding and the locus of divine address\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical — literal is the physical organ; metaphorical is the subtle spiritual reality (laṭīfah rabbāniyyah) that is the locus of knowledge and will\n- Root: حسد (hasad)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Envy; the wishing for removal of another's blessing\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance — envy is the desire for the removal of a blessing from one who deserves it, sometimes accompanied by striving to remove it; the believer envies (ghibṭah) while the hypocrite envies (hasad)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical — \"Envy consumes the body as fire consumes wood\"; the envier is one who wishes evil upon the blessed\n- Root: هلك (halak)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Perdition, destruction, ruin; death\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance — three senses: (1) loss of a thing from one's possession while it exists elsewhere, (2) corruption and dissolution of a thing, (3) death; the Quran uses it for all three\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical — literal is physical destruction; metaphorical is the ruin of one's religion and afterlife through spiritual corruption\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of interposing elements between a preposition and its object when the intervening material constitutes a complete, meaningful unit, as in the Quranic pattern of extended periodic sentences that delay resolution for rhetorical effect.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Condemnation of Envy'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "7": [{"arabic": "وهي الأبواب المفتوحة إلى نار الله تعالى الموقدة التي تطلع على الأفئدة كما أن الأخلاق الجميلة هي الأبواب المفتوحة من القلب إلى نعيم الجنان وجوار الرحمن والأخلاق الخبيثة أمراض القلوب وأسقام النفوس إلا أنه مرض يفوت حياة الأبد وأين منه المرض الذي لا يفوت إلا حياة الجسد ومهما اشتدت عناية الأطباء بضبط قوانين العلاج للأبدان وليس في مرضها إلا فوت الحياة الفانية فالعناية بضبط قوانين العلاج لأمراض القلوب وفي مرضها فوت حياة باقية أولى وهذا النوع من الطب واجب تعلمه على كل ذي لب إذ لا يخلو قلب من القلوب عن أسقام لو أهملت", "english": "To proceed: Good character (al-khuluq al-ḥasan) is the attribute of the Master of the Messengers and the most excellent of the works of the Truthful Ones (al-ṣiddīqīn). It is, in truth, half of religion and the fruit of the striving (mujāhadah) of the God-fearing and the discipline (riyāḍah) of the devout worshippers. Evil character traits are the deadly poisons, the destructive ruinous things, the disgraceful shames, the manifest vices, and the foul impurities that distance one from the proximity of the Lord of the Worlds and that string their possessor into the ranks of the devils. They are the open doors leading to the Fire of God Almighty, the kindled one {«الَّتِي تَطَّلِعُ عَلَى الْأَفْئِدَةِ»} — \"which rises over the hearts\" — just as beautiful character traits are the open doors from the heart to the bliss of the Gardens and the proximity of the Most Merciful.", "book_title": "Breaking the Two Desires", "section_title_ar": "Section 612", "section_title_en": "The Book of Training the Soul, Refining Character, and Treating the Maladies of the Heart — Being the Second Book of the Quarter of the Destroyers", "section_index": 612, "anchors": "- Root: خلق (khalaqa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of measuring, proportioning, and fashioning; also the innate disposition and character (khuluq) that is the form of the soul.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخلق أصله: التقدير المستقيم\" — the original sense is sound estimation/proportioning; used for bringing something into being from nothing (ibdāʿ) and for the inward character that is the soul's stable form.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"خلق الخراز الأديم\" — the leather-worker measures the hide before cutting. Metaphorical (majāz): \"خلق الله الخلق: أوجده على تقدير أوجبته الحكمة\" — God created creation upon a measure necessitated by wisdom. Thus \"khuluq\" (character) is the soul's measured, stable form.\n- Root: مرض (mariḍa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sickness, departure from the body's balanced temperament.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"المرض: الخروج عن الاعتدال الخاص بالإنسان\" — sickness is departure from the equilibrium specific to the human. Two kinds: bodily sickness, and \"عبارة عن الرذائل كالجهل، والجبن، والبخل، والنفاق\" — an expression for vices such as ignorance, cowardice, miserliness, and hypocrisy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: bodily illness. Metaphorical: \"مرض في الأمر: ضجع فيه\" — to be weak/lax in a matter; and \"تمارض\" — to feign illness. Thus heart-sickness is the soul's departure from its proper spiritual equilibrium.\n- Root: قلب (qalaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn, overturn, transform from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — turning a thing and diverting it from one face to another. The heart (qalb) is so named for its constant turning and fluctuation between states.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" — turned it from its face. Metaphorical: \"قلب التاجر السلعة\" — the merchant examined the merchandise, scrutinizing its conditions. Thus the heart is the seat of moral scrutiny and transformation.\n- Root: حسن (ḥasuna)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Beauty, goodness, excellence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحسن: عبارة عن كل مبهج مرغوب فيه\" — goodness is an expression for everything delightful and desirable. Three kinds: good by reason, good by desire (passion), good by sense.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"أنظر إلى محاسن وجهه\" — behold the beauties of his face. Metaphorical: \"فلان لا يحسن شيئا\" — so-and-so does not do anything well; \"وقيمة المرء ما يحسن\" — a man's value is what he does well. Thus ḥusn al-khuluq (good character) is the soul's excellence in its measured form.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the permissibility of interposing elements between a governing word and its governed prepositional phrase, as in \"كقبح رب فيها رجل\" — demonstrating that what may be separated in eloquent speech follows the pattern of what is grammatically sound and rhetorically effective.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Breaking the Two Desires'", "badge": "Insight"}]}, "wordTiming": {"minshawi": {"1": [[1, 220, 1603], [2, 1603, 2848], [3, 2848, 3871], [4, 3871, 5130]], "104:1": [[1, 220, 1603], [2, 1603, 2848], [3, 2848, 3871], [4, 3871, 5130]], "2": [[1, 310, 1439], [2, 1439, 2203], [3, 2203, 3943], [4, 3943, 5660]], "104:2": [[1, 310, 1439], [2, 1439, 2203], [3, 2203, 3943], [4, 3943, 5660]], "3": [[1, 330, 1820], [2, 1820, 2725], [3, 2725, 5053], [4, 5053, 6450]], "104:3": [[1, 330, 1820], [2, 1820, 2725], [3, 2725, 5053], [4, 5053, 6450]], "4": [[1, 20, 1715], [2, 1715, 4421], [3, 4421, 5230], [4, 5230, 6940]], "104:4": [[1, 20, 1715], [2, 1715, 4421], [3, 4421, 5230], [4, 5230, 6940]], "5": [[1, 280, 2356], [2, 2356, 3689], [3, 3689, 4357], [4, 4357, 5350]], "104:5": [[1, 280, 2356], [2, 2356, 3689], [3, 3689, 4357], [4, 4357, 5350]], "6": [[1, 200, 1329], [2, 1329, 2289], [3, 2289, 4150]], "104:6": [[1, 200, 1329], [2, 1329, 2289], [3, 2289, 4150]], "7": [[1, 20, 1338], [2, 1338, 2863], [3, 2863, 3943], [4, 3943, 5500]], "104:7": [[1, 20, 1338], [2, 1338, 2863], [3, 2863, 3943], [4, 3943, 5500]], "8": [[1, 320, 2355], [2, 2355, 4026], [3, 4026, 5930]], "104:8": [[1, 320, 2355], [2, 2355, 4026], [3, 4026, 5930]], "9": [[1, 320, 1125], [2, 1125, 2131], [3, 2131, 4220]], "104:9": [[1, 320, 1125], [2, 1125, 2131], [3, 2131, 4220]]}, "abdulbasit": {"1": [[1, 600, 1210], [2, 1220, 2230], [3, 2240, 3190], [